     1	# Chapter 1 — Winter's End
     2	
     3	*Hundred and thirty-first day past the Line. Lowmarch.*
     4	
     5	*The ferry went west yesterday with the Line's lead on the sack and our two letters in it, and this morning the skin ice on the shallows had not broken where the bow broke it, because nothing went out to break it. The ferryman has taken his ropes in. He says the river will be a road by the month's end and a river again in five, and that between the two it is nothing anyone should be on. So: five months. I am dating the winter by the Line because the Line is the only thing out here that keeps a count, and I am writing down the day the count stopped moving.*
     6	
     7	*Inventory, because I do one at the start of anything, and a winter is a thing. I am nineteen. Ten confirmed. The still place: not a fragment, not the Quiet, read three times, and not touched since a healer said stop. Ninety-six in hand. The inn's six, for the back room where the sheets are, came out of the hundred and two before the snow, and is not coming out again. Nothing owed. Three hundred entered on Pike's sheet as taken and never to be paid, which is a kind of money too.*
     8	
     9	*No tier. No rank. No file I can see. I checked from the landing this morning, both directions, out of habit, and there was snow both directions, and a ferryman with his ropes.*
    10	
    11	*Hundred and thirty-third day.*
    12	
    13	*The two inns have priced the winter. The upper inn wants a mark a head a week and the lower inn wants the same, and Pike, who keeps the board's room at the lower inn and has chosen the smoke over the cold, said to the book without looking up, "Meadow rate is four a month for a crew that's on my sheet," and the woman who keeps the lower inn said, "Pike," and Pike said, "Eleventh," and that was the price: four a month for the five of us, twenty for the five months, paid in front, which leaves seventy-six. Brom paid it out of the pouch and counted it twice and the woman counted it once, and the five of us have a room with a floor and a fire and a door that shuts, and Karis has the back room for the sheets, and the boxes are under the bed where the mule can't get at them, and the mule is in the ferryman's shed for a mark a month that he has not asked for and will.*
    14	
    15	*Teague's crew have the upper inn. Four of them and the healer of no Path, at the table by the window, with the winter road's pay on it. Teague looked across the yard at the five of us going in under our own roof and lifted his chin a finger's width, which is the whole of the etiquette between crews in a town with one street, and is, in that town, a sentence. His term stands: spring, whatever's on that bearing, his crew comes. He said it at the plank and I said the one word back, and the season line above our names stayed blank, which was a thing Pike had done on purpose, and a thing Pike does on purpose is a thing.*
    16	
    17	*Hundred and fortieth day.*
    18	
    19	*Pike's board in winter. Eleven slips where there were forty. A roof. A well-house. Wood cut and stacked for the upper inn. A fence, ours, forty, held over from the autumn. Nothing that needs a Path and nothing that would let you use one, and the board does not care, because the board has never once asked what a crew was and is not going to start when the river is a road.*
    20	
    21	*We walked the fence. Two days out on the winter road to the axe-head hold, with the ground iron underfoot and the posts Teague's crew had set in the autumn standing in a line to where they had stopped, and Brom read the line like a wall, and said, "Straight," and we set the rest. Forty-one posts. Seln found the last two of Teague's posts a hand off true and said nothing to anyone but Brom, and Brom reset them, and that is the kind of thing that goes in a ledger nobody reads. The hold's factor paid forty at the gate, in silver, and wrote it in a book of his own, and did not ask for a name, because the slip said Pike's five and that was the name.*
    22	
    23	*A hundred and sixteen in hand. Fence: paid. Pike wrote PAID and the five names under it and the line under that, and that was the tenth line under our names on his page, and he did not say tenth, because the season line above it is still blank, and he was not going to write a number under a blank he had left on purpose.*
    24	
    25	*Hundred and sixty-first day. The river is a road.*
    26	
    27	*Carts on it. Not many. A man walked a mule across this morning where the ferry ran in the autumn, and the ferryman watched him do it from his door with his arms folded, like a man watching somebody use his house without asking.*
    28	
    29	*The kit is on the table.*
    30	
    31	*It has been on the table since the first week, because Karis put it there, and nobody has moved it, because moving it would mean deciding whose it was, and it is not anyone's; it is the Compact's, recovered under a contract that paid nothing, and it sits on a table in an inn where the Compact has never been and is not going to come. The case, road-made, registry-stamped. The pacing cord, knotted at the fives, twenty strides to a cord, and the cord's end-loops worn to the shine that a thing gets when one pair of hands has used it for a season. The stake — thirty-two, by its number — with its point still bright from the twice it was driven at nothing. The mallet. The field ledger, in the surveyor's square hand, with the radius line in it, and the one entry that says what the Stair is — a designation, in an index, that nobody at this table can read — and under it the line that stops: third site confirmed on bearing.*
    32	
    33	*Karis has been learning it. Not the index; the kit. She says a tool teaches you the hand that used it, and that she wants to know that hand better than she knows her own before she meets it, and I do not ask her where she thinks she is going to meet it. She has paced the meadow with the cord in the snow, and the yard, and the inn's long room, and laid the two straightedges on the carter's map every night and moved them and laid them again, and they do not stop touching all four, and she does not stop laying them.*
    34	
    35	*Hundred and sixty-eighth day.*
    36	
    37	*Something to write down.*
