     1	# Chapter 1 — The Count Stopped Moving
     2	
     3	The keeper would not have a mule under his eave for a whole cold term, and he said so at the inn's door, with his cloth over his shoulder, while five people were still looking at a healer's letter on a strip of bark.
     4	
     5	"She's a good beast, and I've nothing against her. But she's stood on my step two nights, and I've a step to sweep."
     6	
     7	So Cael took her down to the river later that same morning, the hundred and thirty-first past the Line, through a hand's depth of new snow.
     8	
     9	She was a small brown mule, and she was not his. She belonged to that healer, who was two days off up a shut road, and who had lent her to five people at a hold's gate a long while ago without saying for how long. He led her by the cheek-strap across the landing's planks to the round shed where the ferry's gelding had walked his circle through all the dry months. The gelding was in the smith's stable now, under a blanket. The shed stood empty, with reed hurdles tied between its posts against the wind, and a great wooden drum in the middle of it that had nothing to wind.
    10	
    11	There was a stone trough inside the door, and the water in it had shut in the night.
    12	
    13	He stood over it, and Ember made its offer. It was the fifth of the ten things that he carried, and it would set one small hot point on whatever he looked at for long enough; it was looking at the ice now, through his eyes, like a child at a window. He told it no. A point was a thing spent, and what it burned was the hand that carried it. He put his heel through the ice instead, twice, and the mule pushed past his knee and drank.
    14	
    15	He was nineteen, and he had no Path. Everybody else whom he knew had been given one at fourteen, at a Kindling, when an Arbiter woke on a brass plate and named the single gift that a person would be ranked by for the rest of her life. His own Arbiter had woken too. It had taken eleven seconds over him, and had gone dark, and it had never lit again. What answered him instead were fragments: pieces of other people's Paths, ten of them now, each one taken in the middle of a fight that somebody had meant, and each one written down in an old man's book with a name beside it and a price.
    16	
    17	The prices were what he knew best. For a hundred and thirty days he had carried all ten down every road in this country and used them as seldom as he could, since the roads out here belonged to things that hunted whatever a Path gave off. He could have said to a grain what each of them charged him for being kept shut. He knew a good deal less about what each would do when it was let out, and where it would fail him, and what it cost afterward.
    18	
    19	It was a poor thing to be ignorant of, with what lay behind him. There were three places in that country, a ring of stones and a stair under a floor and a hall in a marsh, where a line lay on the ground and every Arbiter that crossed it stopped; the fragments had gone on answering him inside all three, and nobody alive could say why. There was a fourth, twice the size, behind a fence of registry iron, which no one had entered. And up at the inn, on a calfskin, a ruled pencil line ran through the middles of all four and off the edge.
    20	
    21	He left the mule to her water and went out to the end of the planks.
    22	
    23	The river had begun to shut. It ran dark and quick in the middle, as it always had, but the shelf along each bank had grown another yard since the ferry crossed. Beyond the shelf, on the slack water, a skin had formed in the night, grey and thin, with the snow lying on it in patches.
    24	
    25	He opened the read.
    26	
    27	It was the third of the ten, and Brom's, and the cheapest thing that he owned. It went out from the soles of his boots for a pace and a half and told him the mass of whatever lay within that distance, and which way the mass was going, and nothing more. It could have the ground or it could have the air, and never both. He gave it the ground. There were the planks, heavy and still on their piles. There was the shelf, one thick slab frozen fast to the bank, which sent its weight home into the gravel like a well-set stone. Past the shelf's edge he could only just find the skin. It weighed almost nothing, and it had nowhere to send even that; it lay on the water as a leaf lies.
    28	
    29	"She'll not bear a cat before the new moon."
    30	
    31	The ferryman was sitting on a keg in the lee of his rope-house, with a running block across his knees and a pot of tallow beside him. He had been there the whole time. He had watched a stranger stable a mule and break a trough and walk to the end of a landing, and he had not thought any of it worth a word until now.
    32	
    33	"I wasn't going to try her," said Cael.
    34	
    35	"You'd the look. I've had that look off carters for twenty cold terms, and I've fished half of them out." He worked tallow into the sheave with his thumb. "The shelf comes first. Then that skin thickens, and takes the snow, and the snow keeps it warm, which is the worst that can happen to ice. Then one night it goes blue, and you'll hear it go, up at the inn. After that she's a road, and they'll drive carts down her."
    36	
    37	"How long?"
    38	
    39	"A month. Less, if it holds as cold as this." His eyes went to the shed's door, where a brown rump showed. "That's a mule in my shed."
    40	
    41	"The inn won't have her."
    42	
    43	"No. He's a step to think of."
    44	
    45	He said nothing more about it. Cael had been priced by experts for as long as he could remember, and he waited for the figure; the man only turned the block over and began on its other cheek.
    46	
    47	Cael stayed where he was, with the cold coming up through the planks, and did what he did at the start of anything, which was to count what he had.
    48	
    49	He had kept five counts since the Line, in Hesk's book, and every one of them had stopped at this river. The ferry had made her last crossing, and her cable hung bare from bank to bank with rime on it; no post would come over until the thaw, so that a letter to Denvash and another to a yard in a western city would lie unanswered for as long as the ice chose. The board was ruled off. There were nine lines under five names in a one-armed man's ledger, and a sheet of sailcloth by the inn's hearth with those names on its eleventh line, and neither would change by a stroke while the holds were shut. There were ninety-six marks in a purse. There were twenty-seven uses written against the ten fragments.
    50	
    51	Nothing was going to move any of those figures for a long while. He had never in his life been anywhere for a whole season with nothing to be measured against, and he did not know what a person did with that.
    52	
    53	He knew what he would do with the first night of it.
