   125	Then she took the leaf out of her coat. *Thirty-one, and a piece under half.* The cord had not altered by the width of her pencil in fifty-four days. She did the second knot, and the third, and they were as the first.
   126	
   127	So the cord was true. She had known that it would be; she had watched four people get one figure out of it in a yard. She had measured it regardless, since *I had known that it would be* was not a sentence that she allowed in a box.
   128	
   129	She sat back upon her heels.
   130	
   131	The two edges were sound as well. She had laid each to its own pricks six times since the hundred and sixty-eighth, at different hours, with her hands warm and with them cold, and they had not once shown her a hair. They agreed with one another, and with the table. There was nothing whatever the matter with any of the three things, and it was their soundness that she could not put down.
   132	
   133	She had a sentence for occasions of this kind. It was the one that she had given him in the yard, as a rule, and she had been leaning her weight on it for half her life.
   134	
   135	She had not made it for brass. She had made it as a girl, in a hall with a counter in it, on the day when a registrar laid a card before her that said *Iron*, and a numeral, and *certified*. The girl beside her had wept at hers. Karis had read her own twice, and had asked what instrument it had been taken with, and how that instrument was kept true; and the registrar had looked at her as at a child who inquires what the sky is fastened to.
   136	
   137	Nobody had been able to tell her. And so she had worked it out for herself on the walk home, and had found that it steadied her wonderfully. The card was not a fact about Karis. It was a fact about a plate, and a table of figures, and whoever had last adjusted them. A ruler said what a ruler said. If it said different things of the same length, then somebody had a bad ruler, and the length was exactly what it had been before.
   138	
   139	She had built a good deal on that. She had gone into rooms where she was the smallest person and the lowest tier, and had argued from it. She had copied marks off stone for years with nobody's leave, and nobody's belief either. A rubbing was a fact about a wall, and it did not matter in the least whose hand had held the pencil.
   140	
   141	And now there was a cord that read forty-two in four hands and forty-one and a piece in a fifth.
   142	
   143	She made herself look at it squarely, as she would have looked at another person's error. The cord was sound. The yard had not moved. The peg had been hers, and the pebbles. She had set them with her own fingers, the third time, so that he had done nothing at all but hold an end. If her sentence was true, then what she had seen could not have happened. She had seen it three times.
   144	
   145	There was another sentence. She knew that there was, as one knows that there is a step in the dark at the bottom of a stair.
   146	
   147	She could feel every part of it but one. It would begin with a boy in whom no instrument had ever found a tier. It would have three floors in it, where five people's Arbiters stopped at a line and something of his did not. It would have a cord, and a hair. And somewhere in the middle of all those nouns it would need a verb, to say what the one had to do with the others.
   148	
   149	She had not got the verb. She had four observations and no tie between any two of them that she could have defended to a first-year. There were a dozen verbs that would have gone into the place, and each of them would have turned four true things into one thing that she had made up.
   150	
   151	She did not write it. She did not let it finish itself in her head, either, which was harder, at that hour, with the fire sinking behind the wall and four people breathing in the next room. She had struck larger sentences out of better scholars' work, and she had never yet been wrong to.
   152	
   153	On the trials leaf, under the figure for the cord, she entered what she had.
   154	
   155	*Hundred and sixty-eighth: one cord, two edges. Four hands agree. One does not, by a piece and by a hair. Cord re-measured on the hundred and seventieth: sound. Cause: not found. No inference entered.*
   156	
   157	She read that over, and left it. It was true, and it was very much less than she wanted.
   158	
   159	---
   160	
   161	It was past the middle of the night when she opened the other book.
   162	
   163	She had meant to go to her blanket. She had got as far as putting the cord away, coiled, in its place in the tarred case, beside a stake that had never been in ground; and then her hand had gone to the limp little ledger instead of to the lamp.
   164	
   165	It fell open at its first page. It always did, now.
   166	
   167	There were the four signs, and the number.
   168	
   169	She had looked at them on every night since the ice road, and they had not improved. They were small, and upright, and they pressed, like everything in that hand; but they were not his own. A man does not invent four signs for the place where he is standing. He had copied them out of something, carefully, as a carter copies the mark of a hold onto a bale. They were somebody's name for a square hole with a stair in it.
   170	
   171	And under the number, where there was no need for anything at all, he had put the short stroke and the bar.
   172	
   173	She knew that mark from four places. It was struck into the head of the stake in that case. It was on the newer of two iron bars at a grey stone on the Stair road. It was on every bar of a fence round a place that she had not been let into, under the number thirty-four. And it was here. It stood beside the Stair twice over and beside the fourth place, and she had never found it beside the Fallow or the hall.
   174	
   175	She had turned that over until it was as smooth as a river stone. Two had the mark, and two had not. The two that had it were not the two largest, since the Stair was of one size with the others. They were not the two nearest to anything. A girl with her arm in a sling had pointed out, on a morning by a marsh, which way the numbers ran, and Karis had entered the order and declined to say what was being ordered. She declined still. She had been at that mark for fifty-five days, and she could not have said what it counted, or whether it counted anything.
   176	
   177	But somebody could.
   178	
   179	It was this that she could not let go, and she knew it for what it was. She had spent the whole of her working life drawing answers out of stone a stroke at a time, by argument, against people who did not wish to be argued with. Every one of those answers was hers, and every one was only as strong as her reasoning. Here was a thing of another kind. A clerk had sat at a desk, with a book open, and had looked up what sort of place the Long Stair was, and had found it. He had written it down in four signs and two strokes of a punch. He had not derived it. He had been told.
   180	
   181	There was a page somewhere on which those signs stood in a column, with their meanings beside them, in plain writing, for the use of clerks.
   182	
   183	She had never in her life wanted an object as she wanted that page. It was not only that it would save her a year. She had been right before, in an annex, with three leaves on a trestle before it was light; she had been right, and it had made no difference, since she had had nothing to show a seat but her own rubbings and her own word. Stone proved that marks existed. It took a text to say what they meant, and it took a text that somebody else had kept to make anybody believe her.
   184	
   185	She knew where such texts were kept. She had known for years, having once read the place's name on the spine of a catalogue that she was not allowed to open. It was a long way from this room. It was on the other side of a river, and of a Line, and of a great deal that five people had walked away from; and there was one among the five whom it would cost more than all the rest together.
   186	
   187	She would have to ask it of him. She understood that tonight, with the dead man's four signs under her hand, as she had not let herself understand it at the fence.
   188	
   189	She would not ask until she could say the whole of it at once, and say what it rested on, and say what it did not. She had been giving it to them in pieces because the pieces were all that she had. A person does not ask a friend to walk into the place that he fears most on the strength of a piece.
   190	
   191	She took a clean leaf, and wrote a heading across it in her small exact hand.
   192	
   193	*What I should have to be able to show.*
   194	
   195	Under it she began a list. The lamp burned down a finger's breadth before she had the fourth line, and she set a second point on the wick without noticing that she had done it.
   196	
   197	---
   198	
   199	The long dark lay on Lowmarch through all the weeks that followed, when the sun stood up over the eastern hill for five hours and went home; and it was in those weeks that Cael filled the last of his seven lines.
   200	
   201	He laid the lane on the hundred and seventy-sixth night, in the meadow below the ash, when the three fires were covered. Storm was the seventh of the ten, a Gold's worth of weather that had come to him out of a bout with a Gold, and it was the heaviest thing that he carried. It wanted the air. To give it the air he had to take the ground from the read, since at that weight he could have one or the other; and it was like shutting an eye.
   202	
   203	The lane went down the snow toward the river, as long as a cart and team, high at his end and low at the other. Everything in it went one way. A flood of dry snow poured along that channel as meal pours out of a sack, and burst over the bank in a white cloud.
   204	
   205	Then he paid, as he had paid for every lane that he had ever laid. For ten counts all that he had came to him a beat behind. Lira threw a ball of snow at him at the fourth count, by arrangement. He saw it strike his chest, and afterward he saw it coming.
   206	
   207	"Ten," she said. "And clean on eleven. It's what you wrote."
   208	
   209	"It's what it's always been. Ten for the first, ten for the second, twelve for the third." He stamped his feet until they were his own again. "I've never laid a fourth on top of three, and I'm not going to find out what it costs in a field at night, beside a river, for a page."
   210	
   211	Anchor he tried in the shed, on the capstan bar. He set a point with his palm, and held it, and the other nine went thin and far off, as they had in a drowned hall. Brom leaned his good shoulder on the bar and could not stir it. At thirty Cael let go. He had been gripping the drum with his other hand, to stay upright, and had not known it. Afterward he set a second, and took it down with his hand on it, as he had once taken down another person's at a knot. He paced off from a third to learn where it left his sight. It was sure at ten feet, and faint at twelve, and at fourteen there was nothing there.
   212	
   213	*Storm, from Daeva: one lane. Ten counts late; clean at eleven. The read goes blind to the ground while it lasts. Three in a row is as many as I have laid.*
   214	
   215	*Anchor, unnamed: a point, by the hand, held to thirty. Everything else goes while it holds. Seen at ten feet; not at fourteen. Trials. The two uses stand at two.*
   216	
   217	*That is the ten. Deployment since the Line: twenty-nine.*
   218	
   219	The silent yard went on through all of it, on every morning that the cold road spared, and he lost there more often than he won.
   220	
   221	He learned something from each losing. But what he learned most from was a game that Teague set on the hundred and ninetieth day, with a sack of meal in the middle of the square and three people at either end. Whoever carried it over the other side's pegs had it. Cael stood with Seln and Karis, since they were the three of the five who had two arms apiece; and he turned to them to say what he thought they should do.
   222	
   223	He had said four words of it when the sack went past him.
   224	
   225	Teague's three had not spoken, and their captain was not even among them. He stood by the bench with his thumbs in his belt. He had looked at a place on the trodden snow, once, before the word was given; and when it was given the spear was standing in that place, and the bow was where the spear had been, and the boy had the sack over the pegs and was waiting to be told whether he might put it down.
