   243	The light had come back before the warmth did. Then the warmth came, by day, and the eaves ran. At night it froze again, so that every morning the meadow was a sheet of glass with last year's grass standing up through it. Lira walked on it with her foot set down whole and her eyes on the ground. She had not fallen since a road in the frost, and she did not mean to begin; and each evening she went down to the gravel bar and stood looking at the saddle to the east, where the snow still lay to the top.
   244	
   245	The ferryman had his scow up on blocks by then, and was paying her seams with tar.
