   254	
   255	"And there's a mule."
   256	
   257	"I know that there's a mule," said Brom.
   258	
   259	Cael had not touched the cord again. Karis had not offered it, and he had not asked; it lay coiled in its tarred case beside a stake that had never been driven, and one pale hair lay pressed in the fold of a notebook, and for ninety-five days the two of them had kept his ruling as carefully as if either had known what it was a ruling about.
   260	
   261	Karis said the other thing on the two hundred and sixty-third.
   262	
   263	She had asked Cael into the store behind the chimney, and shut the door, which she did not do.
   264	
   265	"I am going to say a thing to you whole, before I say it to anybody else. You are the person whom it costs."
   266	
   267	"You've been saying it since the fence."
   268	
   269	"In pieces. I had not the right to say all of it until I had looked in every other place where the answer might lie, and I have looked. It is not in fifty-two sheets. It is not in that case, or in his book." She had a leaf before her with a heading on it and eleven lines. She did not read from it. "Whoever cut those courses did not think in stone. Stone is where a thing is put when it is finished. It was written first; and what is written is kept; and the oldest keeping that I know of is on the far side of that river, inside a wall, under an office of the registry."
   270	
   271	He had known for a long while that the sentence would end so. It did not help him at all to have been right.
   272	
   273	"And somebody would have to walk in."
   274	
   275	"Somebody would. I am asking nothing of you tonight. I am telling you what I shall be arguing, and from what, so that you need not hear it for the first time in front of the others."
   276	
